A||||5||terryk||3.1L/3.4L Iron Head Piston Part numbers and DOHC||||1||
Z||000000||terryk||12-16-1999||03:07 PM||||For those looking to swap the crank in their 2.8L to a 3.1L, here is the Silvolite piston part number needed to maintain 8.9:1 compression. These are cast (like stock) and not recommended for amounts of significant boost, but great for standard 3.1L builds and mild boost. <P>Silvolite PN 1486 $23.38 each (local CA cost).<P>At least you can avoid using the 8.5:1 Camaro pistons (unless you plan on boost, then the Camaro's are a better choice).<P>For 3.4L iron head replacement pistons, the part number is PN 3420 $25.56. These 8.9:1 also. <P>For you 3.4L DOHC freaks, the part number is PN 3409 $25.52.<P>TK||||reg||1
Z||000001||Eric||12-16-1999||05:10 PM||||I wish I knew my compression for sure.  I was told the Federal Mogul H562P pistons out of a '90 Camaro were 9.0:1 compression, but they had a large dish to them that was about 2mm deep.  The guy at Summit told me there was two compression ratios available for that engine, naturally I ordered the higher one.  I think they're good pistons, hyperutectic alloy, ceramic coated skirts, all the good stuff, but maybe they're a little low on squeeze.  What kind of dish do you have on those 8.9:1 compression pistons, Terryk?  Part of me hopes they are the lower compression version because I hope to someday install a turbo and it would be good to know I could pump up the boost without overstressing the engine.  For now it seems to run well.  It has a lot of torque, but think it capable of making a few more ponies.||||reg||1
Z||000002||fierosound||12-16-1999||07:18 PM||||Here's another combination that is supposed to work...  a 3.4L OHV block with iron heads using 3.4L DOHC pistons.  This is suppoed to produce about 9.7:1 C.R.  Check these websites.  I think I may use this combination when I get started with my 3.4  Has anyone else done this???<P> <A HREF="http://www.angelfire.com/on/freddysplace/NewProject.html" TARGET=_blank>www.angelfire.com/on/freddysplace/NewProject.html</A> <BR> <A HREF="http://www.angelfire.com/on//freddysplace/shortblk.html" TARGET=_blank>www.angelfire.com/on//freddysplace/shortblk.html</A> <P><BR>||||reg||10
Z||000003||batboy||12-16-1999||11:47 PM||||Be warned, if you run more than about 9:1 CR with cast iron heads, you'll have to always use premium gas. If you run more than 9.5:1 CR, you might have to use an octane booster in the gas which is a pain in the hinny.||||reg||1
Z||000004||terryk||12-17-1999||10:08 AM||||I would call Federal Mogul directly. The stores are all over the map on the CR's for pistons. I was refering to stock flat top replacement pistons (expect for the DOHC's which have valve reliefs.)<P>As far as I know, the Camaro 3.1L only had 8.5:1, but FM could easily produce a higher CR piston, so call them.<P>Yes, there are several other combinations that will work.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||Eric||12-17-1999||06:27 PM||||I doubt my pistons are 9.0:1 compression, maybe close.  The dish is too big.  Even with the extra stroke I don't think it's much over 8.5:1 compression.  In any case, it burned the 87 octane I had in the tank without any sign of detonation.  I think I'll stick with 89 though, atleast for now.  I put 92 in the other day.  I don't know, it's too early to tell.  We'll see how it runs at its first oil change.||||reg||1
